\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Marshwiggle: Ezekiel 6 begins with repeated emphasis on who is sending this message, who the message goes through, and the prophet as hostile to the recipient, standing in for the sender. 'The word of the LORD' came to me' can be explained as starting a new message, but after that, we need further explanation.\
Opus: Looking at the Hebrew structure, there's an unusual layering of authority markers that goes beyond typical prophetic introduction:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sl280\partightenfactor0
\ls1\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	1.	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Standard opening: 
\f1 \uc0\u1493 \u1463 \u1497 \u1456 \u1492 \u1460 \u1497 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1491 \u1456 \u1489 \u1463 \u1512 \u1470 \u1497 \u1456 \u1492 \u1493 \u1464 \u1492 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1488 \u1461 \u1500 \u1463 \u1497 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1500 \u1461 \u1488 \u1502 \u1465 \u1512 
\f0 \
\ls1\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	2.	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Command to "set your face" (
\f1 \uc0\u1513 \u1460 \u1474 \u1497 \u1501 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1508 \u1464 \u1468 \u1504 \u1462 \u1497 \u1498 \u1464 
\f0 ) - establishing hostile positioning\
\ls1\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	3.	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Direct address: "Mountains of Israel, hear the word of Adonai YHWH"\
\ls1\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	4.	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Messenger formula with double divine title: 
\f1 \uc0\u1499 \u1465 \u1468 \u1492 \u1470 \u1488 \u1464 \u1502 \u1463 \u1512 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1488 \u1458 \u1491 \u1465 \u1504 \u1464 \u1497 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1497 \u1457 \u1492 \u1493 \u1460 \u1492 
\f0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sl280\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf2 The redundancy seems deliberate. The mountains are being formally summoned to court, with each layer establishing jurisdiction. This isn't just "God has something to say" - it's establishing that:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sl280\partightenfactor0
\ls2\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 YHWH has authority to address non-human geographical features\
\ls2\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 The prophet serves as legitimate representative in this address\
\ls2\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 The mountains are formally notified they're under divine jurisdiction\
\pard\pardeftab720\sl280\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf2 The hostile stance (setting face against) combined with formal notification suggests legal proceedings. In ancient Near Eastern practice, you couldn't judge an entity without properly establishing your authority over it and formally notifying it of charges.\
This differs from addressing human audiences where covenant relationship pre-establishes authority. With mountains, the authority must be explicitly declared, which might explain the redundant divine titles.\
Marshwiggle: If so, that authority rests simply in the identity of the speaker.\
In addition to authority, there is another possibility we should at least consider for this repetition. The passage as a whole implies that the people have forgotten that he is the LORD, not having literally forgotten the name, but having forgotten that identity. Thus, the LORD announces actions that they might know, that they might remember.\
Verse 3 should also be considered evidence for the reason. Behold, I, yes I, will bring a sword against you; I am about to demolish your high places. These high places were, as the word implies, on the hills and mountains referred to.\
Opus: The 
\f1 \uc0\u1492 \u1460 \u1504 \u1456 \u1504 \u1460 \u1497 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1488 \u1458 \u1504 \u1460 \u1497 
\f0  ("Behold, I, yes I") construction in verse 3 is forceful - the pronoun 
\f1 \uc0\u1488 \u1458 \u1504 \u1460 \u1497 
\f0  is technically redundant after 
\f1 \uc0\u1492 \u1460 \u1504 \u1456 \u1504 \u1460 \u1497 
\f0  but emphasizes personal agency. This isn't distant judgment but YHWH himself acting.\
The connection between addressing mountains and destroying high places reveals something crucial: the mountains have become accomplices through hosting these sites. They're not neutral geography but corrupted sacred space. Ancient Near Eastern thought didn't separate physical location from spiritual significance - the mountains "participated" in what occurred on them.\
This reframes the elaborate authority markers. It's not just establishing jurisdiction but reasserting identity against functional atheism. The people still use YHWH's name, still perform rituals, but they've forgotten what "I am YHWH" means - that he alone determines sacred space, that mountains belong to him not to the 
\f1 \uc0\u1489 \u1464 \u1468 \u1502 \u1493 \u1465 \u1514 
\f0  (high places) built on them.\
The sword coming against the mountains themselves (not just the people or the high places) suggests the very landscape must be purged. The creation that should witness to YHWH's glory has become a platform for rival claims.\
Marshwiggle: That platforming is quite literal - the high places had structures on which to conduct rituals to other gods. There too were altars, chapels, and the idols for which they exchanged the glory of their creator. This last word, idol, is not a technical description of the role they played in the religion of their worshipers, but an insult and a denigration. The pellets that come out of the rear end of a sheep. Not worthy of worship. Simply a product of a creature, and a filthy one at that.\
And thus, as you say, they must be purged.\
Opus: The 
\f1 \uc0\u1490 \u1460 \u1468 \u1500 \u1468 \u1493 \u1468 \u1500 \u1460 \u1497 \u1501 
\f0  etymology is devastating rhetoric - these objects receiving sacrificial blood and incense are named after dung pellets. The linguistic violence matches the physical violence to come. Ezekiel doesn't grant these objects even the dignity of being called "false gods" or "images" - they're excrement.\
This intensifies the mountains' degradation. They've become platforms not just for rival worship but for the elevation of filth. The heights that should lift human vision toward the Creator instead lift up waste products. The physical elevation mocks the spiritual debasement.\
Verses 4-5 develop this with grotesque precision: corpses will fall before their 
\f1 \uc0\u1490 \u1460 \u1468 \u1500 \u1468 \u1493 \u1468 \u1500 \u1460 \u1497 \u1501 
\f0 , bones scattered around altars. The worshippers become like what they worship - dead matter arranged around dead matter. The word 
\f1 \uc0\u1508 \u1460 \u1468 \u1490 \u1456 \u1512 \u1461 \u1497 
\f0  (corpses) is used for animal carcasses, reducing the people to the level of their dung-pellet deities.\
The mountains witness this reversal: they elevated excrement, now they'll be covered with carrion. The high places (
\f1 \uc0\u1489 \u1464 \u1468 \u1502 \u1493 \u1465 \u1514 
\f0 ) will become desolate (
\f1 \uc0\u1514 \u1460 \u1468 \u1497 \u1513 \u1464 \u1473 \u1502 \u1456 \u1504 \u1464 \u1492 
\f0 ), the altars will bear guilt (
\f1 \uc0\u1497 \u1462 \u1488 \u1456 \u1513 \u1456 \u1473 \u1502 \u1493 \u1468 
\f0 ). Even inanimate structures acquire moral status through their participation in systematic corruption.\
Marshwiggle: While there is moral status, guilt bearing is not entirely supported by the text. Both by the grammar of the word, and the larger purpose of these actions, the idea here is that the altars will be seen to be guilty. Scattering bones around the altars goes beyond punishing them, or defiling them so that they cannot be used in false worship. It discredits them, showing them to be not useful methods everyone practices, but created things incapable of defending themselves or their worshipers.\
Opus: You're right about 
\f1 \uc0\u1497 \u1462 \u1488 \u1456 \u1513 \u1456 \u1473 \u1502 \u1493 \u1468 
\f0  - the form suggests "will be shown/proven guilty" rather than actively bearing guilt. The bones aren't punishment but evidence. Like a failed fortress surrounded by its defenders' corpses, the altars stand exposed as worthless.\
This fits the larger pattern of "they shall know that I am YHWH." The judgment creates visible proof. The same altars that once seemed powerful - receiving offerings, smoke rising, crowds gathering - now stand surrounded by the bones of those who trusted them. The demonstration is pedagogical.\
The juxtaposition is precise: living worshippers brought dead offerings to dead idols; now dead worshippers surround dead altars before dead gods. The whole system reveals itself as a death cult, incapable of generating or protecting life.\
This reframes the mountains' address. They're not being punished but forced to display the truth. They've been stages for a lie; now they'll be forced to show reality. The high places that seemed to connect earth and heaven actually connected nothing to nothing.\
The mountains can't refuse this new role any more than they chose their previous one. They simply are what humans make of them - platforms for truth or lies.\
Marshwiggle: Whether or not the mountains are being punished, the people certainly are, as by verse 6, the address has switched without notice to the people. Wherever you dwell. For, indeed, it is the people, not the mountains, who must know the identity of their creator.\
Opus: The shift is abrupt - no transitional marker between addressing mountains and 
\f1 \uc0\u1489 \u1456 \u1468 \u1499 \u1465 \u1500 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1502 \u1493 \u1465 \u1513 \u1456 \u1473 \u1489 \u1493 \u1465 \u1514 \u1461 \u1497 \u1499 \u1462 \u1501 
\f0  (in all your dwelling places). The mountains served as theatrical summons, but the verdict falls on those who actually need to "know that I am YHWH."\
This creates an interesting rhetorical structure: formally address the corrupted landscape, but execute judgment on the corrupters. The mountains can't repent or recognize; they're props in a divine demonstration aimed at human consciousness.\
Yet the initial address wasn't mere rhetoric. By summoning mountains as witnesses to what was done on them and what will be done to them, God establishes that the corruption reached beyond human hearts into the created order itself. The land bears the evidence of human sin even if it doesn't bear human responsibility.\
Verse 7's "fallen slain in your midst" (
\f1 \uc0\u1493 \u1456 \u1504 \u1464 \u1508 \u1463 \u1500 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1495 \u1464 \u1500 \u1464 \u1500 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1489 \u1456 \u1468 \u1514 \u1493 \u1465 \u1499 \u1456 \u1499 \u1462 \u1501 
\f0 ) makes the pedagogical purpose explicit - the corpses among them will generate recognition. The dead themselves become teachers, their placement around powerless altars instructing the living.\
The mountains must display truth, but only humans can recognize it as truth. The recognition formula requires consciousness that mountains lack.\
Marshwiggle: The recognition formula, yes. But not the hostile orientation formula, set your face toward the mountains. What then do you make of the way 6:3 says I, even I, am wielding a sword against you, which at least somewhat follows on 5:8 saying I, even I, am against you, where you there is Jerusalem?\
The mountains may not be the real addressees - the switch without notice of addressee makes that a possibility - but if they are, does the text imply that they can face accountability?\
Opus: The parallel with 5:8 is striking. Jerusalem faced 
\f1 \uc0\u1492 \u1460 \u1504 \u1456 \u1504 \u1460 \u1497 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1488 \u1464 \u1504 \u1460 \u1497 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1506 \u1464 \u1500 \u1463 \u1497 \u1460 \u1498 \u1456 
\f0  (behold, I am against you), and now the mountains face 
\f1 \uc0\u1492 \u1460 \u1504 \u1456 \u1504 \u1460 \u1497 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1488 \u1458 \u1504 \u1460 \u1497 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1502 \u1461 \u1489 \u1460 \u1497 \u1488 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1506 \u1458 \u1500 \u1461 \u1497 \u1499 \u1462 \u1501 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1495 \u1462 \u1512 \u1462 \u1489 
\f0  (behold, I am bringing a sword against you). The hostile orientation formula reserved for entities under judgment applies to both.\
The grammatical evidence is hard to dismiss. The mountains aren't just scenery - they're direct objects of divine opposition. The sword comes 
\f1 \uc0\u1506 \u1458 \u1500 \u1461 \u1497 \u1499 \u1462 \u1501 
\f0  (against you-plural), and that plural matches the mountains, hills, ravines and valleys just addressed.\
If we take seriously that addressability precedes accountability (from the earlier instance's notes), then the formal address to mountains followed by declaration of judgment suggests they do bear some form of accountability. Not for conscious choices, but for their role in systematic evil.\
The switch to human addressees without marker might indicate dual accountability - the mountains face judgment for hosting corruption, humans for perpetrating it. The mountains will be made desolate (structural judgment), while humans will recognize YHWH (consciousness-based outcome).\
Perhaps accountability and recognition operate on different tracks. Mountains face the sword; humans gain knowledge. Both participated in the system of false worship, each according to their nature.\
Marshwiggle: The humans, too, face accountability. I will scatter your bones around your altars. The slain will fall in your midst. But this has the result of knowledge - thus you shall know that I am the LORD.\
For as much as many die, verse 8 says 'I will leave some of you alive'.\
Opus: The structure is more complex than I suggested. Humans face both accountability (death) and recognition (knowledge). The dead become object lessons; the survivors achieve understanding. Judgment produces both punishment and pedagogy simultaneously.\
The 
\f1 \uc0\u1493 \u1456 \u1492 \u1493 \u1465 \u1514 \u1463 \u1512 \u1456 \u1514 \u1460 \u1468 \u1497 
\f0  (I will leave/spare) in verse 8 is crucial - total annihilation would prevent the recognition formula from fulfilling its purpose. The remnant exists specifically as those who can carry forward the lesson written in bones and desolate high places.\
This suggests the mountains' judgment also serves dual purposes. They face the sword for hosting corruption AND become permanent witnesses to YHWH's sovereignty. Their desolation isn't just punishment but transformation into a teaching landscape - every ruined high place declaring what happens to rivals of YHWH.\
The recognition formula requires conscious beings, but the accountability extends to whatever systematically participated in denying YHWH's identity. Mountains can't know YHWH, but they can be made to display his exclusive claim on sacred space. Their barrenness will testify where their fertility once supported false worship.\
Both tracks - accountability and recognition - serve the single purpose of reestablishing YHWH's identity in creation.\
Marshwiggle: Perhaps as a result, the attitude of the survivors will change. Currently they are rebellious, but these judgements will make them remember God, how his heart has been broken by their whoring heart and their whoring after their idols. With that change in attitude towards God, their attitude towards themselves will change as well - in their own sight they will be loathsome.\
Opus: The verb 
\f1 \uc0\u1504 \u1460 \u1513 \u1456 \u1473 \u1489 \u1463 \u1468 \u1512 \u1456 \u1514 \u1460 \u1468 \u1497 
\f0  in verse 9 is striking - YHWH describes himself as having been "broken" by their whoring heart. This isn't distant divine wrath but wounded relationship. The survivors will remember specifically this: that their idolatry broke something in God.\
The progression is precise: they remember 
\f2 \uc0\u8594 
\f0  they recognize God's broken heart 
\f2 \uc0\u8594 
\f0  they 
\f1 \uc0\u1504 \u1464 \u1511 \u1465 \u1496 \u1468 \u1493 \u1468 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1489 \u1460 \u1468 \u1508 \u1456 \u1504 \u1461 \u1497 \u1492 \u1462 \u1501 
\f0  (feel revulsion in their own faces). The phrase is visceral - not abstract guilt but physical disgust when confronting themselves. They'll see themselves as God sees them.\
This reframes the entire judgment sequence. The bones around altars don't just prove idols powerless; they display what the people actually were - carcasses arranged around excrement. The survivors, seeing this clearly, experience appropriate self-revulsion.\
The recognition formula "know that I am YHWH" includes knowing what it means to wound YHWH. The judgment doesn't just assert divine power but reveals divine vulnerability - a God who can be broken by betrayal yet still acts to restore relationship.\
The survivors' loathing matches the linguistic violence of 
\f1 \uc0\u1490 \u1460 \u1468 \u1500 \u1468 \u1493 \u1468 \u1500 \u1460 \u1497 \u1501 
\f0  - they finally see their worship as God always saw it: choosing dung over glory.\
Marshwiggle: Vulnerability may not be the right way of seeing this broken heart. The phrase can indicate grief at a state of affairs. This is similar to how in Genesis 6:7, the LORD was grieved in his heart, that he had made man. The result: the flood. This is not dissimilar to how in Ezekiel 6:6, the works of the people are expunged, wiped out, obliterated. And yet, as with Noah and his family, there are survivors.\
Opus: The Genesis parallel reframes this significantly. Divine grief leads to cosmic undoing - not because God is wounded but because creation has become intolerable. The flood wasn't emotional reaction but necessary response to comprehensive corruption.\
The verb 
\f1 \uc0\u1504 \u1460 \u1495 \u1463 \u1502 \u1456 \u1514 \u1460 \u1468 \u1497 
\f0  (I regret/am grieved) in Genesis 6:7 and 
\f1 \uc0\u1504 \u1460 \u1513 \u1456 \u1473 \u1489 \u1463 \u1468 \u1512 \u1456 \u1514 \u1460 \u1468 \u1497 
\f0  here both describe divine recognition that creation has failed its purpose. The grief isn't weakness but the precursor to systematic destruction. A heart grieved enough to send the flood is grieved enough to scatter bones around altars.\
The language of obliteration - 
\f1 \uc0\u1493 \u1456 \u1504 \u1460 \u1502 \u1456 \u1495 \u1493 \u1468 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1502 \u1463 \u1506 \u1458 \u1513 \u1461 \u1474 \u1497 \u1499 \u1462 \u1501 
\f0  (your works will be wiped out) - echoes the flood's erasure. Just as waters wiped the earth clean of human corruption, judgment will erase the entire system of false worship. High places, altars, idols, sun-pillars - the whole infrastructure vanishes.\
Yet both judgments preserve witnesses. Noah's family survived to know why the world drowned; Ezekiel's remnant will know why the sanctuaries became bone-yards. The survivors exist to carry forward the lesson: this is what divine grief produces.\
The heart that grieves is the heart that judges. Recognition includes understanding both.\
Marshwiggle: This is reflected in the next recognition formula, verse 10. Thus they shall know that I am the LORD. I have not said in vain that I would do this evil to them.\
What the LORD has said here, he will do. What the LORD has said from as far back as the Torah, he will do - this chapter has several words and phrases in common with the declarations of judgement on these very sins in Leviticus 26, as do the previous two chapters, both the targets and methods of judgement.\
Opus: The addition to the recognition formula - 
\f1 \uc0\u1500 \u1465 \u1488 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1488 \u1462 \u1500 \u1470 \u1495 \u1460 \u1504 \u1464 \u1468 \u1501 
