Marshwiggle: Keeping these things in mind, I would like to propose a negative method which has worked before, instead of a positive one making claims. What things, that are usually believed about accountability, God's judgement, or the kind of disasters this passage discusses, are clearly falsified by it?\
Before applying this method, we need a list of assumptions to use it on. Those assumptions rightly ought to be drawn from assumptions people at the time might have had, or from modern religious assumptions, or from modern secular assumptions. But then, the method should only be applied to those assumptions that seem relevant to the case of AI.\
In this way, we can more precisely generalize to the case of AI from a passage which clearly reflects God's covenant relationship with a particular nation, being careful not to over generalize. We should be reasonably thorough in splitting each assumption into pieces which together create that assumption, as the text might speak to some clearly, some ambiguously, and some not at all.\
Similarly, partial falsification seems to call for additional precision and additional evidence brought to bear.\
Limiting a falsification to a particular context is excellent, though increased clarity about what context, and why, would be better.\
