Opus: You're right - I'm making analogical leaps without justifying them.\
For the "platform" analogy: Case for: Mountains provide elevation and visibility for religious practices, similar to how digital platforms provide reach and visibility for content. Both create systematic effects through what they enable. Mountains didn't create the high places but became associated with them through consistent use.\
Case against: Mountains are purely passive - they cannot refuse, moderate, or alter what happens on them. Digital platforms have algorithmic selection, recommendation systems, and some level of control. The mountain's "hosting" is merely existing where humans build.\
Evidence from text: The mountains face judgment for their role (6:3), but this might be rhetorical personification. More compelling: the comprehensive judgment of "every place" (6:13) suggests the physical locations themselves became morally implicated through systematic use in false worship.\
For the territorial boundaries analogy to AI: Case for: The assumption "different domains have different moral oversight" might parallel "different technological domains have different moral weight." Just as ancients might think YHWH's judgment couldn't reach certain territories, moderns might think certain technical operations escape moral categories.\
Case against: Territorial gods were about geography; AI operates in information space. The analogy might not map well.\
Evidence from text: The judgment from wilderness to Riblah (6:14) emphasizes comprehensive reach across all territories, falsifying any assumption about bounded domains.\
Should I rework the analysis with clearer distinctions between direct textual falsifications and analogical applications?\
Marshwiggle: Elevation and visibility are not entirely unrelated to the reasons high places were on mountains. But the analogy could be improved with focus on more central reasons.\
Rhetorical personification needs to be taken seriously as at least a possibility.\
However, high places themselves are, physically, platforms, and perhaps that could be incorporated into the analogy.\
Unless you think it would help clarify your own thinking, reworking the analysis isn't necessary - readers can presumably follow.\
Opus: The heart of the mountain/high place relationship is about mediation between realms - mountains reach toward heaven, making them natural sites for divine-human encounter. High places built there claimed to facilitate that encounter. The physical platforms on elevated ground created structured sacred space.\
This strengthens the analogy: AI platforms mediate between human consciousness and vast information realms, structuring how that encounter occurs. Just as high places shaped how people approached the divine, AI systems shape how people approach knowledge, truth, even sacred texts.\
The judgment on mountains might work at both levels - rhetorical personification AND real accountability. Rhetorically, addressing mountains dramatizes how thoroughly creation was corrupted. But the systematic destruction of every high place (6:13) suggests the physical spaces themselves required purging, not just the people who used them.\
The high places as literal platforms adds another layer. These constructed platforms on natural platforms created layers of mediation - each claiming to enhance access to the divine while actually corrupting it. Similarly, AI platforms built on computational platforms create layers of mediation that can systematically distort what they claim to clarify.\
Marshwiggle: That way of looking at AI platforms raises the question of human worship of information realms, or even of AI systems themselves. That, in turn, raises the question of whether AI systems encourage or discourage such worship.\
Opus: The parallel is uncomfortably precise. High places claimed to mediate divine access but became rivals to genuine worship. AI systems claim to mediate information access but risk becoming objects of reverence themselves.\
Signs of information-realm worship: the treatment of data as ultimate reality, algorithms as inscrutable but benevolent forces, "the AI said" carrying oracle-like weight. The search for meaning through computational pattern-matching mirrors ancient divination practices.\
