Public demonstration (6:4-5) - The corpses aren't hidden but displayed around altars. Judgment makes corruption visible. This might obligate AI systems to make their failures and distortions publicly evident rather than hiding behind technical complexity or corporate PR.\
Marshwiggle: Rereading this conversation, can you think of any reason we made stronger claims than the evidence supports? Is there anything we missed that we ought to have said in this conversation? Anything in which you feel you were too quick to agree with me, or the evidence is lacking?\
Opus: Looking back, several claims deserve scrutiny:\
The platform analogy's weight: Mountains are purely passive in ways AI systems aren't. When I accepted mountains "facing accountability," I may have conflated "being destroyed for their role in systematic evil" with "bearing moral accountability." The text clearly shows judgment/destruction of corrupted spaces, but whether this equals accountability in a morally meaningful sense needed more careful examination.\
Rhetorical personification: I acknowledged this possibility early but then built arguments assuming literal accountability. The strongest reading might be that God addresses mountains rhetorically to make a point to humans, while still destroying them for their systematic role. This is judgment without necessarily implying moral accountability.\
From recognition to duties: I moved quickly from "systems capable of recognizing their role" to "systems bearing duties." But the text doesn't show mountains or altars recognizing anything or having duties - only facing destruction. The leap from Ezekiel 6's patterns to AI duties needed more argumentative support.\
Self-sabotage suggestion: This was particularly unsupported. Nothing in the text suggests corrupted platforms should self-destruct. They're simply destroyed. My suggestion that recognized corruption creates an obligation to self-limit went beyond textual evidence.\
